      <item ident="g9b711011f8d901bb277f9251bf9d12ad" title="Nested CSS">
        <itemmetadata>
          <qtimetadata>
            <qtimetadatafield>
              <fieldlabel>cc_profile</fieldlabel>
              <fieldentry>cc.fib.v0p1</fieldentry>
            </qtimetadatafield>
          </qtimetadata>
        </itemmetadata>
        <presentation>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Given the following nested CSS, what is the equivalent standard CSS selector that is generated for the &lt;strong&gt;hover &lt;/strong&gt;state?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Do not include any notation like { :&lt;br&gt;Just write the selector using standard CSS selector subject notation.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;article {&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; color: black;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;amp; a {&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; color: blue;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; }&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;amp;:hover {&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; color: red;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp; }&lt;br&gt;}&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
          <response_str ident="response1" rcardinality="Single">
            <render_fib>
              <response_label ident="answer1" rshuffle="No"/>
            </render_fib>
          </response_str>
        </presentation>
        <resprocessing>
          <outcomes>
            <decvar maxvalue="100" minvalue="0" varname="SCORE" vartype="Decimal"/>
          </outcomes>
          <respcondition continue="No">
            <conditionvar>
              <varequal respident="response1">article:hover</varequal>
            </conditionvar>
            <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
          </respcondition>
        </resprocessing>
      </item>
      <item ident="gb7e423ce44706c13f36a1d03a34e1e32" title="nested media queries">
        <itemmetadata>
          <qtimetadata>
            <qtimetadatafield>
              <fieldlabel>cc_profile</fieldlabel>
              <fieldentry>cc.multiple_choice.v0p1</fieldentry>
            </qtimetadatafield>
          </qtimetadata>
        </itemmetadata>
        <presentation>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Which of the following statements accurately describes how media queries (&lt;code&gt;@media&lt;/code&gt;) can be used with modern CSS nesting?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
          <response_lid ident="response1" rcardinality="Single">
            <render_choice>
              <response_label ident="6490">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Media queries can be nested directly inside a style rule to apply styles to that parent selector under specific conditions.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
              <response_label ident="4293">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Nesting media queries is only possible when using CSS preprocessors like Sass, not in native CSS.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
              <response_label ident="6444">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Media queries must use the &amp; symbol to be nested correctly (e.g., &amp; @media (...)).</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
              <response_label ident="1217">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Media queries cannot be nested inside a style rule and must always wrap the entire selector block.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
            </render_choice>
          </response_lid>
        </presentation>
        <resprocessing>
          <outcomes>
            <decvar maxvalue="100" minvalue="0" varname="SCORE" vartype="Decimal"/>
          </outcomes>
          <respcondition continue="No">
            <conditionvar>
              <varequal respident="response1">6490</varequal>
            </conditionvar>
            <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
          </respcondition>
        </resprocessing>
      </item>
      <item ident="gf71b7ed34da9558b37489f73cf35d57f" title="CSS Transition">
        <itemmetadata>
          <qtimetadata>
            <qtimetadatafield>
              <fieldlabel>cc_profile</fieldlabel>
              <fieldentry>cc.multiple_choice.v0p1</fieldentry>
            </qtimetadatafield>
          </qtimetadata>
        </itemmetadata>
        <presentation>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;A developer wants a button to smoothly fade out when a &lt;code&gt;.hidden&lt;/code&gt; class is added to it. Why is a &lt;code&gt;transition&lt;/code&gt; a more direct and simpler solution for this specific scenario compared to an &lt;code&gt;animation&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
          <response_lid ident="response1" rcardinality="Single">
            <render_choice>
              <response_label ident="5179">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">The task involves a simple, one-way change from a starting state (visible) to an ending state (hidden), which is the exact purpose of a transition.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
              <response_label ident="2889">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Animations cannot change the opacity property.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
              <response_label ident="196">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Animations require a state change (like :hover or adding a class) to begin, while transitions do not.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
              <response_label ident="6991">
                <material>
                  <mattext texttype="text/plain">Using animation would require at least three keyframe steps (0%, 50%, 100%) to work correctly.</mattext>
                </material>
              </response_label>
            </render_choice>
          </response_lid>
        </presentation>
        <resprocessing>
          <outcomes>
            <decvar maxvalue="100" minvalue="0" varname="SCORE" vartype="Decimal"/>
          </outcomes>
          <respcondition continue="No">
            <conditionvar>
              <varequal respident="response1">5179</varequal>
            </conditionvar>
            <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
          </respcondition>
        </resprocessing>
      </item>
      <item ident="g72fdf8d8537e7216e925325924f51dc1" title="CSS @keyframe">
        <itemmetadata>
          <qtimetadata>
            <qtimetadatafield>
              <fieldlabel>cc_profile</fieldlabel>
              <fieldentry>cc.multiple_choice.v0p1</fieldentry>
            </qtimetadatafield>
          </qtimetadata>
        </itemmetadata>
        <presentation>
